# Data and operation design

- Rust contracts own domain records, selectors, defaults, validation and receipts.
  ETL normalizes provider vocabulary once, preserves attributed observations and
  publishes through KnowledgeRepository. Disposable query projections consume
  those records; they never invent a second signature, identity or type system.
- Separate exact references from discovery selectors. Reuse entity kinds,
  explicit text match modes and exact/subtree/glob path selectors. A signature
  requirement binds its fields to one parameter; multiple requirements disclose
  whether they require distinct parameters. Written syntax is not resolved type
  identity, assignability, argument binding or runtime behavior.
- Share matching and edge admission implementations across operations. Endpoint
  selection, returned-endpoint filtering and traversal pruning have different
  meanings. Apply pruning during both frontiers, seed discovery and shortcut
  interiors; include every semantic filter in cache and continuation identity.
- Preserve snapshot, build context, source commitment, provenance and ambiguity
  through extraction, storage/reload, queries and both frontends. Unsupported or
  missing observations remain explicit; counts describe their examined universe.
- Separate graph work, result selection, evidence detail and response delivery.
  State deterministic ordering and witness selection. Output pagination never
  advances discovery. Return usable typed follow-ups for ambiguity and limits.
- CLI and MCP lower into the same typed requests and validation. Expose ordinary
  flags for common choices and typed request input for composition. Keep narrow
  task tools; reject irrelevant fields instead of silently ignoring them.
  Preserve existing spellings or document an intentional compatibility change.
- Prefer Rust standard collections/algorithms, established permissively licensed
  crates and database indexes to custom commodity implementations. Check current
  maintenance, license, platform/MSRV support, Unicode and bounded-work behavior.
  Measure representative cost and compare results independently before replacing
  an optimization. Keep Entmoot-specific scope/evidence semantics at their owner.
- Extend existing owners before introducing wrappers, query languages, schedulers
  or retained state. Adopt shared primitives only where semantics agree; domain
  operations retain their distinct invariants (including test ownership floors).
- Validate required distinctions through production fixtures, persistence/reload
  and CLI/MCP parity. Cover malformed selectors, missing facts, alternate routes,
  parallel occurrences, changed filters on resume and stale publication fences.
  Regenerate schema/examples; update owning documentation and test ownership.
